Cactus Code Thorn TestSubcyclingMixedProlong
Author(s)    : Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Maintainer(s): Liwei Ji <jiliwei.phys@gmail.com>
Licence      : LGPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Regression test for subcycling-in-time when two RK-integrated groups of
one centering use different prolongation operators.

Under subcycling, a coarse level keeps "source bands" (the old state and
the RK stage derivatives) on the coarse cells underneath its child's
coarse-fine ghost footprint, and the child evaluates the RK dense output
from them into a per-group coarse patch that it then prolongates. The
dense-output kernel walks the bands and the coarse patch with one box
index, so both must have the same layout. The width of that layout
depends on the group's prolongation stencil (on vertex-centred data one
coarse point of padding for "ddf" order 1, three for "hermite" order 5),
hence the band geometry must follow the group, not only its centering.

This thorn is a trimmed copy of TestSubcyclingMC2 (scalar wave equation
against the analytic Gaussian solution, fourth-order centred Laplacian,
ODESolvers RK4, three refinement levels) with two vertex-centred state
groups that solve the same problem and differ only in their tags:

  state_lo {u_lo, rho_lo}   prolongation_type="ddf"     prolongation_order=1
  state_hi {u_hi, rho_hi}   prolongation_type="hermite" prolongation_order=5

Both tags are explicit, so the driver-wide CarpetX::prolongation_type of
the parameter file is irrelevant.

The narrow group is declared first on purpose. Cactus numbers the groups
of a thorn in declaration order, and a driver that shares the band
geometry per centering takes it from the group with the lowest index.
With the narrow group first the shared band is narrower than the coarse
patch of the wide group. That was the failure mode of the driver that
shared the band geometry: its layout assertion in the RK boundary fill
fired, and in a build without assertions (NDEBUG) the kernel read outside
the band's boxes, which poison_undefined_values turned into a visible
failure. With the wide group first the assertion would have fired too,
but an NDEBUG build would have been silently correct, so that order could
not pin the defect in every build. The driver no longer has that
assertion: the bands and the coarse patch of a group are now allocated
together from one FPinfo (EnsureRKBuffers in CarpetX/src/subcycling.cxx),
so the per-group geometry is structural, and this test guards it.

2. Tests

  test/gaussian.par   3 levels, RK4, use_subcycling = yes, ghost_size = 3,
                      poison_undefined_values = yes; outputs both state
                      groups and both error groups every 64 iterations up
                      to t = 2
